✅ Reproducibility status
At this stage:

the article and final DOI are available;
the figures have been recovered and stored in images/;
the bibliography has been archived in Zenodo;
the website structure is reproducible with Quarto;
the original complete analysis code is not assumed to be preserved.

Future reconstructed scripts should therefore be versioned transparently and documented as reimplementations based on the published methodology.

🎯 Research objective
The study evaluates the strengths and limitations of classical and quantum machine-learning approaches in a high-variability urban environmental setting.
The empirical analysis focuses on:

temporal PM2.5 behaviour;
LSTM forecasting performance;
QSVM threshold classification;
computational time and memory usage;
the potential complementarity of classical and quantum models.

⚛️ Quantum model: QSVM
The quantum experiment reformulates the problem as binary classification using a 25 µg/m³ threshold.
The implementation described in the article uses:

Qiskit;
ZFeatureMap;
three qubits;
depth 2;
angle encoding;
a fidelity-based quantum kernel;
and a classical statevector simulator.






Fig. 3. Distribution of real PM2.5 concentrations by predicted QSVM class.
The article reports perfect classification metrics in the evaluated experiment and a training time of 0.08 s, with peak memory usage of 54.89 MB.

📓 Original notebook
The recovered Google Colab notebook should be preserved unchanged in:
notebooks/
└── 2025_Mathematics_Computers_recovered.ipynb
It contains original material for:

PM2.5 data preparation;
exploratory analysis;
QSVM/Qiskit experimentation;
classification metrics;
and supporting figures.

No complete original LSTM implementation was recovered from the notebook.

🧠 Reconstructed LSTM
The LSTM reconstruction follows the model-specific configuration reported in the article:

Plaza Castilla PM2.5 time series;
training period 2017–2023;
external test period 2024;
one LSTM layer with 64 units;
dense output layer;
50 epochs;
Adam optimizer;
MSE loss;
MinMax normalization.

Published metrics used as reference:
RMSE = 15.98 µg/m³
MAE  = 4.42 µg/m³
R²   = 0.05
The reconstructed script is not claimed to reproduce those values exactly because the full original execution environment and preprocessing implementation were not preserved.

⚛️ Recovered QSVM
The recovered notebook preserves a Qiskit-based quantum classification workflow.
The article describes:

ZFeatureMap;
three qubits;
depth 2;
angle encoding;
fidelity-based quantum kernel;
statevector simulation;
25 µg/m³ binary threshold.

The recovered code relies on a legacy Qiskit API and may require an environment compatible with the versions used during the original experiment.

🧠 LSTM model
The LSTM architecture is presented as the classical sequence-based model.





Fig. 4. LSTM cell structure and example PM2.5 prediction.
The published implementation specifies:

one LSTM layer;
64 units;
dense output layer;
50 epochs;
Adam optimizer;
MSE loss;
MinMax normalization to [0,1].

The dedicated LSTM section states that data from 2017–2023 were used for training and 2024 as an external test set.
